      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense—it's a much more coherent way to view their position if we assume they are prioritizing a transcendent order over empirical observation.\n\nbut if we follow that logic to its conclusion, doesn't that create a fundamental epistemological impasse? if one side defines \"truth\" as something that is necessarily static and insulated from biological reality, isn't their theology essentially opting out of the modern intellectual community? \n\neven if we grant them the \"feature\" of protecting the sanctity of life, how can we view their framework as a serious contributor to global bioethics if it's structurally designed to be immune to the very scientific discoveries that inform our understanding of personhood, agency, and suffering? doesn't that effectively render their theological input obsolete in a practical, policy-making context?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
